{"name":"Bloody Safflower","hex":"#c53d43","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bloody","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Covered in blood.","Characterised by bloodshed."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Used to express anger, annoyance, or shock, or for emphasis."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bloody"},{"term":"safflower","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A cultivated thistle-like plant, Carthamus tinctorius, family Asteraceae, now grown mainly for its oil."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/safflower"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bloody","blocks":[{"text":"Bloody, as an adjective or adverb, is an expletive attributive commonly used in British, Irish, Australian and New Zealand English. It is also used in Canada, India, Hawaii, Kenya, Malaysia, New Caledonia, Singapore, South Africa, Zimbabwe and a number of other Commonwealth nations. It has been used as an intensive since at least the 1670s."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bloody","wikidata":"Q4928046"},{"title":"Safflower","blocks":[{"text":"Safflower, also false saffron, is a highly branched, herbaceous, thistle-like annual plant in the family Asteraceae. It is one of the world's oldest crops; today, it is commercially cultivated for vegetable oil extracted from the seeds. Plants are 30 to 150 cm tall with globular flower heads having yellow, orange, or red flowers. Each branch will usually have from one to five flower heads containing 15 to 20 seeds per head. Safflower is native to arid environments having seasonal rain. It grows a deep taproot which enables it to thrive in such environments."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Safflower.jpg/330px-Safflower.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Safflower","wikidata":"Q156625"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Illustration_C